  "configuration_id": "config_024",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best-performing configuration so far is config_022 (multimodal, singleprofile, PCA n2000, ViT-L, concat) at 0.535 acc / 0.519 F1, showing that multimodal fusion with ViT-L provides a meaningful ~5-point boost over RNA alone (0.485). Concat and weighted fusion are nearly equivalent (0.535 vs 0.532), so fusion type is a low-priority variable. However, all five completed experiments used singleprofile; the multiprofile dimension is entirely unexplored and represents the largest remaining source of uncertainty. Question: Does aggregating multiple cellular profiles (via avgpool) on top of the best singleprofile multimodal setup yield further gains? Why this experiment: config_024 is the minimal, clean extension of the top configuration—same encoders (PCA n2000 + ViT-L), same fusion (concat, marginally better than weighted), but with multiprofile + avgpool. Using avgpool (the simplest aggregator) isolates the effect of multiprofile itself; if it helps, a follow-up with transformer aggregation becomes justified, and if it doesn't, we learn that singleprofile suffices and can redirect budget toward RNA encoder alternatives (SCVI, SCGPT). This maximizes information gain per experiment under the limited budget."
}

